  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 405 AM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nThe latest guidance continues to show a highly amplified long wave \npattern over the CONUS at least through Friday. The upper trough \ncontinues drifting east through midweek which allows temperatures \nto continue to warm back into the mid and upper 80s by Wednesday.  A \nshort wave moves through the Midwest into the Great Lakes and Ohio \nValley by late Wednesday afternoon/evening.  This reorients the long \nwave trough back to the west and pushes a cold front south through \nthe Mississippi Valley.  Models print out light QPF ahead of the \nfront Tuesday night and Wednesday, however the forcing isn't \nparticularly strong Tuesday night, and elevated instability is \nlimited.  While CAPE increases to 2000+ J/Kg ahead of the front on \nWednesday, there's enough CINH ahead of the front to make precip \nchances questionable.  The going 20-30 PoPs look reasonable given \nthe cap and relatively weak forcing, but if the cap breaks there \ncould certainly be more widespread thunderstorms.  Deep-layer wind \nshear will be around 30kts, so any storms that do form could be \nstrong to severe.\n\nThe airmass behind the cold front isn't that much cooler, so highs \nThursday and Friday will likely only be 2-3 degrees cooler than \nWedneday's.  However it does look drier, so lows drop as much as 5-7 \ndegrees back into the upper 50s and low 60s across much of the area \nby Friday morning.  The long wave pattern begins to attenuate \nFriday, and by Saturday the flow aloft is quasi-zonal. Deterministic \nguidance is pretty insistent that temperatures will rise back into \nthe upper 80s and low 90s Saturday and some locations will see mid \n90s by Sunday afternoon.  The LREF agrees, and even the lower \nquartile high temperatures are in the low to mid 90s Sunday \nafternoon.  That being said, the IQR is fairly wide at 6-8 degrees. \nWith that in mind confidence in the temperature forecast for \nSaturday and Sunday isn't particularly high.",
